<h2>Why the notice exists</h2>
<p>In <em>Arnesh Kumar v. State of Bihar</em> (2014) 8 SCC 273 — a case arising from a Section 498A IPC arrest — the Supreme Court held that arrest in the up-to-seven-year band must be the exception, justified on a recorded checklist, and that magistrates authorising detention must verify that the checklist was honestly applied. <em>Satender Antil v. CBI</em> (2022) 10 SCC 51 institutionalised the discipline, and the Supreme Court has since clarified in the continuing Satender Antil proceedings that the notice must be served in the prescribed manner — service through instant messaging applications is not a substitute. Police officers who arrest in breach of the regime risk departmental action and contempt; detentions authorised in breach expose the remand itself to challenge.</p>

<h2>Responding to a notice: a practical sequence</h2>
<div class="flow">
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Read the FIR first.</strong> The notice names the FIR and police station. Obtain the FIR — it is ordinarily available online, and otherwise through an application — and identify the offences and their maximum punishments before the first appearance.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Appear, with a record.</strong> Attend on the date fixed or seek a short deferment in writing. Keep an acknowledged copy of every appearance and every document supplied; the trail of cooperation is the foundation of later bail arguments.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Answer, do not volunteer.</strong> The appearance is for investigation, not adjudication. Factual answers and documents may be given; confessional statements to police are inadmissible in any event, and argumentative explanations are better reserved for a written representation settled by counsel.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Reassess custody risk continuously.</strong> If the offences are re-cast into a graver band, if co-accused are arrested, or if the officer signals that custodial interrogation is sought, an anticipatory bail application under Section 482 BNSS deserves immediate consideration.</div>
</div>

<h2>The notice and anticipatory bail</h2>
<p>The two protections run on parallel tracks. A Section 35(3) notice reflects the officer&rsquo;s present view that arrest is unnecessary; it is not an undertaking for the future. Courts hearing anticipatory bail pleas routinely weigh whether the applicant honoured such notices — compliance strengthens the plea, evasion undermines it. Conversely, the issuance of a notice does not bar anticipatory bail: where the apprehension of arrest is reasonable, the two can sensibly coexist, with the bail application recording the cooperation already extended.</p>

<div class="check">
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Never ignore the notice</strong> — Section 35(6) makes default itself a ground of arrest.</li>
    <li><strong>Demand recorded reasons</strong> — any arrest in the up-to-seven-year band without them is open to challenge at remand.</li>
    <li><strong>Preserve every acknowledgment</strong> — cooperation must be provable, not merely asserted.</li>
    <li><strong>Senior citizens should invoke Section 35(7)</strong> — the DSP-permission requirement is a statutory safeguard worth citing in representations and bail pleadings alike.</li>
  </ul>
</div>
<div class="note"><p>This article is for general information only and is not legal advice. The appropriate response to a police notice depends on the FIR, the offences alleged and the stage of investigation in each case.</p></div>
